1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,855
Ice floats on water.

2
00:00:01,855 --> 00:00:04,277
Water boils only at a hundred degrees.

3
00:00:04,277 --> 00:00:07,784
And the two strands of your
DNA stay zipped together.

4
00:00:08,184 --> 00:00:09,501
Three different puzzles.

5
00:00:09,501 --> 00:00:11,326
One tiny pull explains all three.

6
00:00:11,776 --> 00:00:13,593
Diya sees it in her kitchen.

7
00:00:13,593 --> 00:00:16,350
She drops an ice cube
into a glass of water.

8
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:18,731
Most solids sink in their own liquid.

9
00:00:18,731 --> 00:00:19,892
But the ice floats.

10
00:00:20,392 --> 00:00:22,549
By the end, you will know why.

11
00:00:22,549 --> 00:00:24,554
The answer is a hydrogen bond.

12
00:00:25,204 --> 00:00:26,721
Think of a tug of war.

13
00:00:26,721 --> 00:00:30,982
In a bond, two atoms share a pair of
electrons, like two kids holding one

14
00:00:30,982 --> 00:00:31,882
rope.

15
00:00:31,879 --> 00:00:35,690
If one kid is much stronger,
the rope slides to that side.

16
00:00:36,090 --> 00:00:37,426
Atoms do the same.

17
00:00:37,426 --> 00:00:41,617
The pull an atom has on shared
electrons is called electronegativity.

18
00:00:42,017 --> 00:00:46,046
The strongest pullers are
fluorine, oxygen and nitrogen.

19
00:00:46,046 --> 00:00:47,311
F, O and N.

20
00:00:47,711 --> 00:00:49,763
Now join hydrogen to fluorine.

21
00:00:49,763 --> 00:00:53,256
Fluorine pulls the shared
pair far away from hydrogen.

22
00:00:53,656 --> 00:00:58,496
So fluorine gets a small minus charge,
and hydrogen a small plus charge.

23
00:00:58,496 --> 00:01:01,341
We write them delta minus and delta plus.

24
00:01:01,341 --> 00:01:03,183
Delta means a small part.

25
00:01:03,833 --> 00:01:06,836
Now put some HF molecules
on our molecule bench.

26
00:01:07,186 --> 00:01:09,349
Plus attracts minus, like magnets.

27
00:01:09,349 --> 00:01:13,333
So the hydrogen of one molecule
turns to the fluorine of the next.

28
00:01:13,733 --> 00:01:15,552
Fluorine also has lone pairs.

29
00:01:15,552 --> 00:01:19,599
A lone pair is a pair of electrons
that is not shared in any bond.

30
00:01:19,999 --> 00:01:22,600
The plus hydrogen sticks
to that lone pair.

31
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:25,382
This pull between molecules
is a hydrogen bond.

32
00:01:25,782 --> 00:01:27,927
We draw it as a dotted line.

33
00:01:27,927 --> 00:01:31,657
The covalent bond inside each
molecule is a solid line.

34
00:01:32,107 --> 00:01:33,007
Watch.

35
00:01:32,616 --> 00:01:34,947
HF molecules link into a zig zag chain.

36
00:01:35,347 --> 00:01:41,052
So, a hydrogen bond needs a hydrogen
joined to F, O or N, and a lone pair

37
00:01:41,052 --> 00:01:43,177
on a nearby F, O or N.

38
00:01:43,577 --> 00:01:46,208
It is much weaker than a covalent bond.

39
00:01:46,208 --> 00:01:49,438
It is strongest in solids,
and weakest in gases.

40
00:01:50,088 --> 00:01:52,338
There are two kinds of hydrogen bond.

41
00:01:52,688 --> 00:01:54,954
Intermolecular means between molecules.

42
00:01:54,954 --> 00:01:58,427
Inter, like an inter school
match, between two schools.

43
00:01:58,427 --> 00:02:00,628
Water, HF and alcohols do this.

44
00:02:01,028 --> 00:02:03,482
Intramolecular means inside one molecule.

45
00:02:03,482 --> 00:02:06,002
Intra, like a match inside one school.

46
00:02:06,402 --> 00:02:10,707
In ortho nitrophenol, the OH group
and the NO two group sit side by side.

47
00:02:10,707 --> 00:02:14,078
So the hydrogen sits between two
oxygens of the same molecule.

48
00:02:14,528 --> 00:02:17,546
In para nitrophenol, the two
groups sit on opposite sides.

49
00:02:17,546 --> 00:02:19,960
So its hydrogen reaches
out to other molecules.

50
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:21,260
Pause and think.

51
00:02:21,243 --> 00:02:24,812
Which one boils at a lower temperature,
ortho nitrophenol or para nitrophenol?

52
00:02:28,012 --> 00:02:28,912
Ortho!

53
00:02:28,542 --> 00:02:31,042
Its hydrogen bond is
used up inside itself.

54
00:02:31,042 --> 00:02:34,689
So its molecules hold their
neighbours weakly, and escape easily.

55
00:02:35,339 --> 00:02:36,439
Remember this.

56
00:02:36,439 --> 00:02:38,504
A hydrogen bond is a bridge.

57
00:02:38,504 --> 00:02:43,169
Hydrogen holds one atom by a covalent
bond, and the other by a hydrogen

58
00:02:43,169 --> 00:02:44,069
bond.

59
00:02:44,444 --> 00:02:46,134
Now, one worked example.

60
00:02:46,434 --> 00:02:48,840
Which of these form hydrogen bonds?

61
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:52,164
Methane, ammonia, hydrogen
sulphide and methanol.

62
00:02:52,564 --> 00:02:54,071
Methane, CH four.

63
00:02:54,071 --> 00:02:56,043
Its hydrogens sit on carbon.

64
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:58,035
Carbon is not F, O or N.

65
00:02:58,035 --> 00:02:58,935
So, no.

66
00:02:59,614 --> 00:03:01,339
Ammonia, NH three.

67
00:03:01,339 --> 00:03:05,540
Hydrogen sits on nitrogen,
and nitrogen has a lone pair.

68
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:06,440
Yes.

69
00:03:06,861 --> 00:03:08,972
Hydrogen sulphide, H two S.

70
00:03:08,972 --> 00:03:11,686
Sulphur is not a strong enough puller.

71
00:03:11,686 --> 00:03:12,586
So, no.

72
00:03:13,302 --> 00:03:15,656
Methanol, CH three OH.

73
00:03:15,656 --> 00:03:17,739
Hydrogen sits on oxygen.

74
00:03:17,739 --> 00:03:18,639
Yes.

75
00:03:19,117 --> 00:03:22,351
Proof: water boils at a
hundred degrees Celsius.

76
00:03:22,351 --> 00:03:26,525
H two S, with no hydrogen bonds,
boils at about minus sixty.

77
00:03:27,175 --> 00:03:28,483
Now, back to Diya's ice.

78
00:03:28,833 --> 00:03:32,007
In liquid water, the molecules
tumble and crowd close together.

79
00:03:32,407 --> 00:03:37,638
When water freezes, each molecule locks
into hydrogen bonds with four neighbours.

80
00:03:38,038 --> 00:03:42,491
These bonds hold the molecules apart,
in open rings with empty space inside.

81
00:03:42,891 --> 00:03:45,523
More space, less mass in each cube.

82
00:03:45,523 --> 00:03:49,554
Ice is about zero point nine
two grams per centimetre cube.

83
00:03:49,554 --> 00:03:51,057
Water is about one.

84
00:03:51,457 --> 00:03:53,251
Less dense things float.

85
00:03:53,251 --> 00:03:55,055
So ice floats on water.

86
00:03:55,055 --> 00:03:59,872
Hydrogen bonds also help alcohol
mix with water, and hold DNA strands

87
00:03:59,872 --> 00:04:00,772
together.

88
00:04:01,328 --> 00:04:02,793
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:04:03,143 --> 00:04:07,692
Many students say that the OH bond
inside water is the hydrogen bond.

90
00:04:08,092 --> 00:04:08,992
No.

91
00:04:08,574 --> 00:04:10,572
That OH bond is covalent.

92
00:04:10,572 --> 00:04:15,154
The hydrogen bond is the weaker pull
between one water molecule and the

93
00:04:15,154 --> 00:04:16,054
next.

94
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:17,519
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:17,519 --> 00:04:20,603
JEE Main asked about ortho
nitrophenol in twenty twenty one.

96
00:04:20,603 --> 00:04:23,467
Expect statement questions and
boiling point order questions.

97
00:04:24,067 --> 00:04:25,266
Exam level now.

98
00:04:25,266 --> 00:04:27,713
Which has the highest boiling point?

99
00:04:27,713 --> 00:04:31,389
Water, hydrogen fluoride,
ammonia or hydrogen sulphide?

100
00:04:31,389 --> 00:04:32,638
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:38,038 --> 00:04:39,508
The answer is A.

102
00:04:39,508 --> 00:04:40,408
Water.

103
00:04:40,703 --> 00:04:43,158
H two S has no hydrogen bonds.

104
00:04:43,158 --> 00:04:44,786
So it boils lowest.

105
00:04:45,286 --> 00:04:46,833
Fluorine pulls hardest.

106
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:51,628
But HF has only one hydrogen, so it
makes about two hydrogen bonds each.

107
00:04:52,128 --> 00:04:56,685
Water has two hydrogens and two lone
pairs, so up to four hydrogen bonds

108
00:04:56,685 --> 00:04:57,585
each.

109
00:04:57,241 --> 00:04:59,524
More bonds, higher boiling point.

110
00:04:59,524 --> 00:05:00,706
B is the trap.

111
00:05:01,306 --> 00:05:02,847
Here is your recap.

112
00:05:02,847 --> 00:05:04,430
Screenshot this card.

113
00:05:07,630 --> 00:05:09,816
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:05:10,216 --> 00:05:11,116
One.

115
00:05:10,736 --> 00:05:15,451
Hydrogen must be joined to which
three atoms, to form a hydrogen bond?

116
00:05:16,251 --> 00:05:17,151
Two.

117
00:05:16,522 --> 00:05:18,540
Why does ortho nitrophenol boil
lower than para nitrophenol?

118
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:20,240
Three.

119
00:05:19,824 --> 00:05:21,419
Why does ice float on water?

120
00:05:22,319 --> 00:05:26,793
Next time: why does hot food in a
closed steel tiffin still cool down?

121
00:05:26,793 --> 00:05:29,083
We begin Chapter 5, Thermodynamics.
